<P>"Then what is your purose here?" asked Cheskin, "Just opening wounds?"</P><br><P>Kartum took a bite from his plate and laughed, "Not at all. I just think it’s unusual that Fortune should suddenly fail <B><I>poor</B></I> Eshkin—who seemed so favored by her before."</P><br><P>I carefully set down my sliverware and placed my hands on the table, "Explain that." I stated.</P><br><P>"Well," Kartum smiled and leaned back in his chair. He had the attention of the whole room now. "A monster…that killed your woman and <B><I>then</B></I> lit your house afire? Sounds a bit supect to me."</P><br><P>The weight of Ches’ hand on my shoulder made me suddenly aware that I was standing. </P><br><P>Ches squeezed my shoulder, "Kartum, you are a fool. You have been drinking and you are not welcome here. I don’t care whose nephew you are, such talk is sheer spite. We’ll have none of it here."</P><br><P>But Kartum was on a roll. He knew he was getting to me. Such blatant flaunting of Layla’s memory—to use her to try and hurt me—he could not have picked a better subject.</P><br><P>"I’m merely supposing, Cheskin, that Eshkin had to get the money to build his house from somewhere. Perhaps someone wanted it back? Despite her bad taste in men, I’m certain Layla wasn’t earning her keep on the streets of Qeynos…at least I’m <B><I>fairly</B></I> certain…"</P><br><P>My cup of water was in motion faster than I could stop my arm. Its contents splashed into Kartum’s eyes, blinding him for a moment. In that moment, my fingers laced themselves into his hair and pounded his face into the hard oak of the table.</P><br><P>Sound was uncertain and the room was a haze. I am unclear of the reactions of those around me. All I knew was my rage to exterminate Kartum was not to be denied.</P><br><P>There was no hesitation, as my arms carried out actions, seemingly on their own. Perhaps the creature had left the touch of Innoruk upon me.</P><br><P>I have always been a big man. My time at the forge as a boy had conditioned me with the build of a smith from a young age. Kartum was off-balance, bent over the table, unable to get the leverage to pull himself away as I pressed his face into the plate of food before me. With one hand in his hair, I held the half-elf’s face to the table, and he flailed and gasped for air through the mess of food on my plate. With the other, I slid my table knife under his neck and sawed open his throat.</P><br><P>Blood erupted all over the table, the flood of it carrying away bits of bread and meat as it spread across the wood. Kartum’s gargling cry was choked off by the blood and food. I held his face to the table until his struggles ceased…and his body relaxed into death.</P><br><P>As my rage faded, I became aware of the thick silence that had fallen over the dining hall. Some two dozen of the Jaggedpine Rangers stood staring at the grizzly spectacle before them. There was no time to run for the druids—Kartum was obviously quite dead.</P><br><P>Oddly enough, I felt no remorse, as I had on the day I had battled with the gnoll. The gnoll was, somehow…well…somehow the gnoll was just another soldier, innocent at heart, but fighting the fight he was born to fight. Kartum was an animal. I felt no more remorse than had I just gutted a kill.</P><br><P>Letting the bloody knife clatter to the tabletop, I straightened and wiped the blood from my hands with my napkin. A strange numbness had settled on me. "I guess I had better go tell Master Sureshot about this."</P>         <br>
